compileFloorT -- processingCode is a mark on the floor processCode's drive pushes (SEQ 297 R1/R2).

R3's first question, asked on trunk before the change: does a compile that FAILS leave processingCode
set, silencing every later expression in the file? Measured on trunk 2026-10-05: NO. cfBad's body fails
to parse (processCode reports "parse failed"), processCode restores the global on both arms, CF-1 reads 0
and CF-2's expression runs. So CF-0..CF-2 are pinned GREEN on both trunk and the branch, and say so. Only
the parse-failure arm was driven; refuse() sets a flag and does not unwind, so a refusing compile also
returns through the same restore (read in the source, not driven).

The pop.sh row "CF-2 printed once" is the fixture's H7 witness: with the floor never marked, every
compiled body RUNS while it is compiled and CF-2 prints twice (fleet 942 -> 555 green, measured).

CF-3/CF-4 are P4: processingCodE is now a READ of the nearest drive floor, and a kant write refuses by
name. On trunk the same write SET the global, aCTionXpress stopped running expressions, and the rest of
the file never ran -- CF-3, CF-4 and the sentinel are absent there (born red). On the branch the write
refuses ("processingCodE is read-only"), CF-3 reads 0 and CF-4's expression runs.
